          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Oscar Wilde</strong><br>\nOscar Wilde was born in Dublin, Ireland, in 1854. As the son of a successful surgeon and a poet and Irish nationalist mother, he had a culturally rich upbringing. He excelled at Trinity College, Dublin, before continuing his education at Oxford University. There, Wilde became well known for his role in the aesthetic and decadent movements, advocating for \"art for art's sake.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Wilde's Literary Career</strong><br>\nAfter university, Wilde moved to London to pursue writing. He worked on various forms of literature, including journalism, poetry, essays, and eventually became a celebrated playwright. His wit, flamboyant style, and biting social commentary gained him widespread fame. \"The Picture of Dorian Gray,\" his only novel, was a reflection of his own brand of aestheticism and exposed the hypocrisy of Victorian society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Famous Works</strong><br>\nWilde's most notable plays include \"The Importance of Being Earnest\" and \"An Ideal Husband,\" which satirized the upper-class society of his time. His dialogues often included sharp witticisms that have been quoted extensively. Despite their light-hearted and humorous surface, his works engaged with serious contemporary social and personal issues like identity, love, and the façade of respectability.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
