          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Style and Public Persona</strong><br>\nKnown for his flamboyant dress and sharp wit, Wilde became one of the most photographed celebrities of his time. He used his public persona as a kind of performance art to challenge social norms and highlight the constructed nature of identity. Wilde's style and social commentary influenced fashion, art, and literature during the late 19th century.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Trials and Imprisonment</strong><br>\nWilde's life took a tragic turn when he was convicted of \"gross indecency\" due to his homosexual relationships, which were illegal in England at that time. After a series of trials heavily covered by the media, he was sentenced to two years of hard labor. These events brought about his social downfall and inflicted considerable emotional and physical distress.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Life After Prison</strong><br>\nUpon his release from prison in 1897, Wilde's health had deteriorated, and his reputation was destroyed. He spent his last years in exile in France, financially destitute and emotionally shattered. During this period, he wrote \"The Ballad of Reading Gaol,\" a poem reflecting on the harsh realities of prison life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
