          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Wilde's Death</strong><br>\nOscar Wilde died of meningitis on November 30, 1900, at the age of 46. His last years were marked by poverty and a sense of personal tragedy. Despite his suffering, Wilde's wit remained sharp until the end, evidenced by his reported final words regarding the drabness of his surroundings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Wilde's Legacy</strong><br>\nToday, Oscar Wilde is remembered as one of the most talented playwrights of the Victorian era. His plays continue to be performed worldwide, and his novel and short stories are considered classics. The persecution he faced for his sexuality has also made him an iconic figure in LGBTQ history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Modern Culture</strong><br>\nWilde's life and works have had a lasting influence on literature and culture. His plays and characterizations have shaped the English language and theater, and his personal life and philosophy have contributed significantly to discussions on the role of art and the artist in society, as well as the importance of individualism and authenticity.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
